#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++)
        cout << arr[i];
}

Use `i < 5`. The indices of a 5-element array are 0 to 4, so the loop must stop before `i` reaches 5. With `i < 6` or `i <= 5`, it reads one slot past the end, which is undefined behavior.

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    cout << arr[i];
```

How would you change the loop to print the array backwards?

/debug
int maxOf(int a, int b) {
    if (a > b) return a;
    else if (b > a) return b;
}

Return after the `if` instead of using a second condition:

```cpp
int maxOf(int a, int b) {
    if (a > b) return a;
    return b;
}
```

When `a` and `b` are equal, returning `b` is fine because it holds the same value. Every path now ends in a `return`.

